uid "42098bc3-a0a9-510c-94ee-f777802bb49a" :question/math-academy-id "q-162666" :question/problem "Let $R$ be the set of all $2\\times 2$ matrices of the form $\\begin{bmatrix}{a}^{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix},$ where $a\\in Z.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** For any $A\\in R,$ we have $(-1)\\cdot A\\in R.$\n\n- **II.** For any $A\\in R,$ we have $2\\cdot A\\in R.$\n\n- **III.** For any $A\\in R$ and $\\alpha \\in R$, we have $(\\alpha +1)A=\\alpha A+A.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. For example,the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\in R,$ since $4={2}^{2}$ and $2\\in Z.$ However\n\n$$\n(-1)\\cdot A=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\notin R,\n$$\n\nbecause a negative number ($-4$) can't be a square.\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, consider the matrix $A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$ from $R.$ Then, we have \n\n$$\n2\\cdot A=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\notin R\n$$\n\nbecause the $22$-entry of $2\\cdot A$ is not equal to $1.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any $2\\times 2$ matrix $A=({a}_{ij})\\in R,$ the entries are just real numbers. Thus, for any $\\alpha \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n(\\alpha +1){a}_{ij}=\\alpha {a}_{ij}+{a}_{ij},\n$$\n\nand hence,\n\n$$\n(\\alpha +1)A=\\alpha A+A.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323707 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124628 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cb02b67c-90d9-5baf-b00e-590e9271d89e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Sets Closed Under Addition and Scalar Multiplication"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b16ecbe9-c123-5857-9769-f9dac3faf9b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467328} {:answer/id #uuid "f26b27de-130c-