7fca77-deb6-591f-ae0c-b458de0a03bc" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468387}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "18aef812-0fac-5da2-8d8b-c2702b3daa12" :question/math-academy-id "q-35663" :question/problem "Let $S$ be the set of all polynomials with real coefficients and whose degree is greater than or equal to $2.$  Which of the following statements are true? \n- **I.** $f(x)\\in S,$ where $f(x)=2x.$\n- **II.** For every $p(x),q(x)\\in S,$ we have $p(x)+q(x)\\in S.$\n- **III.** The set is closed under addition.\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Let's verify the statements one at a time.\n\n- Statement I is false. The set $S$ contains only polynomials whose degree is greater than or equal to $2.$ However, \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{deg}⁡(2x)=1\\ne 2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)=2x\\notin S.$\n\n- Statement II is false. There exist functions in $S$ such that their sum is *not* in $S.$ For example, let $p(x)=x+{x}^{2}$ and $q(x)=x-{x}^{2}.$ Notice that both these functions belong to $S$ since $\\operatorname{deg}⁡(p(x))=\\operatorname{deg}⁡(q(x))=2\\ge 2.$ However,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{deg}⁡(p(x)+q(x))=\\operatorname{deg}⁡((x+{x}^{2})+(x-{x}^{2}))=\\operatorname{deg}⁡(2x)=1≱2.\n$$\n\nHence, $p(x)+q(x)\\notin S.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, the set is *not* closed under addition.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329743 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124628 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cb02b67c-90d9-5baf-b00e-590e9271d89e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Sets Closed Under Addition and Scalar Multiplication"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ba58b671-2657-5a03-abe7-c1d0f53623a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467554} {:answer/id #uuid "ba1c3ce4-672a-5a3c-a83d-bb611ddd59bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467555}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ba1c3ce4-672a-5a3c-a83d-bb611ddd59bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467555} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c5d2488-f6b5-5b00-8662-08305a2ef8b5" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468498} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "40ba87ba-c6e2-5de6-b649-e0483026a236" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467556} {:answer/id #uuid "62f2e689-0881-5c43-bed6-64a96561cc4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467557}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "40ba87ba-c6e2-5de6-b649-e0483026a236" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467556} :answer-field/id #uuid "a50d472b-7d51-5fe0-859e-60a2e6abcfa4" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468499} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "54074d74-4264-5c20-8484-753531648f60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467558} {:answer/id #uuid "e7263bac-9438-5bd2-bceb-9ddd2ae62b91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467559}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "54074d74-4264-5c20-8484-753531648f60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186467558} :answer-field/id #uuid "3e58aa32-3f2c-58e2-a22d-6bae8ee75a9b" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468500}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b4fc2576-da47-56c9-aeca-80fed8de957c" :question/math-academy-id "q-43859" :question/problem "Let $W$ be the set of functions $f:R\\to R$ such that $f(0)=1.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** $q(x)\\in W,$ where $q(x)={2}^{x}.$\n- **II.** For any $f(x)\\in W$ and $k\\in R,$ we have $k\\cdot f(x)\\in W.$\n- **III.** The set is closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Let's verify the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $q(x)={2}^{x}\\in W$ because\n\n$$\nq(0)={2}^{0}=1.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. There exists a function in $W$ that has a scalar multiple *not* in $W.$ For example, let $f(x)={2}^{x}$ and $k=2.$ Notice that $f(x)$ belongs to $W$ since $f(0)=1.$ However,\n\n$$\n2\\cdot f(0)=2\\cdot {2}^{0}=2\\ne 1.\n$$\n\nHence, $k\\cdot f(x)\\notin W.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since statement II is false, the set is *not* closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445223 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124628 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cb02b67c-90d9-5baf-b00e-590e9271d89e" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Sets Closed Under Addition and Scalar Multiplication"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e57d8cf-406d-5f34-b194-cd33686b0e87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445209} {:answer/id #uuid "5b92cdf9-7bbc-5a4d-bde4-9015e4683e5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445211} {:answer/id #uuid "7099a231-0e75-582a-b5c5-f1a18019b145" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445212} {:answer/id #uuid "b4dee0f7-a23a-5d52-9410-5af27ff4ea58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445214} {:answer/id #uuid "d02e0e83-d95c-5ac9-a67d-e710736e75a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445217}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d02e0e83-d95c-5ac9-a67d-e710736e75a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445217} :answer-field/id #uuid "5e68d1f8-4799-5165-aca8-846dbe8edad9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445220}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "110b21f6-4be1-556e-b1d2-7f471afa85fc" :question/math-academy-id "q-94645" :question/problem "Let $R$ be the set of all $2\\times 2$ matrices of the form $\\begin{bmatrix}a & b \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},$ where $a,b\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?  \n\nI. ${I}_{2}\\in R$\n\nII. For every $A,B\\in R,$ we have $A+B\\in R.$\n\nIII. The set is closed under addition." :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The set $R$ contains only matrices of the form $\\begin{bmatrix}a & b \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},$ where $a$ and $b$ are real numbers. However, ${I}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$ is *not* a matrix of this form. So, ${I}_{2}\\notin R.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, suppose $A=\\begin{bmatrix}a & b \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\in R$ and $B=\\begin{bmatrix}c & d \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\in R,$ where $a,b,c,d$ are real numbers. Then, \n\n$$\nA+B=\\begin{bmatrix}a & b \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}c & d \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}a+c & b+d \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSince $(a+c)$ and $(b+d)$ are real numbers, we conclude that $\\begin{bmatrix}a+c & b+d \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\in R,$ i.e. $A+B\\in R.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is true, the set is closed under addition.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360473 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124484 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7eb09138-45df-5e6c-87af-772980ff7546" :knowledge-point/title "Verifying Properties Involving Vector Addition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "902db5be-70c1-5674-b997-99068ce5edfd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452029} {:answer/id #uuid "814a1883-467d-5324-b6ef-116433356eb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452030}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "814a1883-467d-5324-b6ef-116433356eb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452030} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c08151d-a778-5873-a9ce-fddc4ab45b26" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452862} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f7a65cf4-7f49-5de1-8fe2-89baa7786a6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452031} {:answer/id #uuid "e089eec9-7866-5f1a-a19f-c3d7e6029a81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452032}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f7a65cf4-7f49-5de1-8fe2-89baa7786a6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452031} :answer-field/id #uuid "f698df9a-9c04-5d46-ad26-9bbf7397619f" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452863} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ecbe2c7-ade2-5481-b820-bfe9f428123f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452033} {:answer/id #uuid "3ff2aef1-031d-5390-a56e-ad3f1cd32bac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452034}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3ecbe2c7-ade2-5481-b820-bfe9f428123f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452033} :answer-field/id #uuid "b85432db-4f8a-59af-af6d-ee5723b311ce" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452864}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "86d246c9-ffba-5c6c-b83e-58058d87e01e" :question/math-academy-id "q-94686" :question/problem "Let $E$ be the set of polynomials of the form $a{x}^{2}+1,$ where $a\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The constant polynomial $1$ belongs to $E.$\n- **II.** For the constant polynomial $1$ and any $p(x)\\in E,$ we have $1+p(x)=p(x).$\n- **III.** There are polynomials $p(x),q(x),r(x)\\in E$ such that $[p(x)+q(x)]+r(x)\\ne p(x)+[q(x)+r(x)].$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the constant polynomial $1$ can be represented as\n\n$$\n1=0\\cdot {x}^{2}+1.\n$$\n\nHence, $1\\in E.$\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, consider the polynomial $p(x)={x}^{2}+1\\in E.$ Then, \n\n$$\n1+p(x)=1+{x}^{2}+1={x}^{2}+2\\ne p(x).\n$$\n\nThe zero-polynomial $0$ is the unique polynomial such that for any $p(x)\\in E$ we have $0+p(x)=p(x).$\n\n- Statement III is false. For any polynomials $p(x),q(x)\\in E,$ we have \n\n$$\n[p(x)+q(x)]+r(x)=p(x)+[q(x)+r(x)],\n$$\n\nsince polynomial addition is associative.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360478 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124589 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "099e7caf-085a-5fd5-ad3d-1ebf189527b7" :knowledge-point/title "Verifying Properties Involving Scalar Multiplication and the Distributive Laws"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cd62d7df-1d3e-5dab-b939-b97d87035195" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452047} {:answer/id #uuid "d107fb09-76a7-5171-93cf-06b1fd198e65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452048}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cd62d7df-1d3e-5dab-b939-b97d87035195" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452047} :answer-field/id #uuid "c9e9157a-abab-567b-bcdd-196042a00252" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452871} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bd65aa3b-de16-52fa-b452-7e6806041ad4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452049} {:answer/id #uuid "e05f5154-0168-511c-b16e-60381e73e48c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452050}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e05f5154-0168-511c-b16e-60381e73e48c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452050} :answer-field/id #uuid "a073394d-9013-5e1c-ae29-d7b1de7ebc10" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452872} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "550f8bb7-32bc-59af-a373-74d049435bbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452051} {:answer/id #uuid "4a9097d3-cae0-5637-8117-2b0fbfe255ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452052}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4a9097d3-cae0-5637-8117-2b0fbfe255ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452052} :answer-field/id #uuid "14dca0d8-f85e-55c6-8fbc-2cd1271ca040" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452873}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "03514ad7-d8b6-5b82-8d41-07ae43d6027c" :question/math-academy-id "q-94723" :question/problem "Let $T$ be the set of polynomials of the form $ax+2,$ where $a\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $2x\\in T.$\n- **II.** The scalar multiplication of polynomials in $T$ is associative.\n- **III.** For any $p(x)\\in T$ and $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R$, we have $(\\alpha +\\beta )p(x)=\\alpha p(x)+\\beta p(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The set $T$ contains only polynomials of the form $ax+2,$ where $a$ is a real coefficient. However, $2x$ is not a polynomial of this form. So, $2x\\notin T.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for any $p(x)\\in T$ and for any fixed $x\\in R,$ the result of $p(x)$ is just a real number. Thus, for any $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\alpha (\\beta p(x))=(\\alpha \\beta )p(x).\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any $p(x)\\in T$ and for any fixed $x\\in R,$ the result of $p(x)$ is just a real number. Thus, for any $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n(\\alpha +\\beta )p(x)=\\alpha p(x)+\\beta p(x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125242 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124589 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "099e7caf-085a-5fd5-ad3d-1ebf189527b7" :knowledge-point/title "Verifying Properties Involving Scalar Multiplication and the Distributive Laws"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b0f6fb82-50a8-5852-bf40-a37479778409" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448804} {:answer/id #uuid "9267a6f1-3504-5a32-96b0-ac1372955011" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448805}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9267a6f1-3504-5a32-96b0-ac1372955011" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448805} :answer-field/id #uuid "84410673-cc05-5a6f-b9ee-75274828699f" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449442} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "abad889d-1c90-5258-9739-fee99698474e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448806} {:answer/id #uuid "25dda0f1-64d9-5c8b-b93e-f7f8e89336b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448807}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "25dda0f1-64d9-5c8b-b93e-f7f8e89336b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448807} :answer-field/id #uuid "32ac74a8-70ee-5e3d-b945-366ffa92576a" :answer-field/key "II" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449443} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a729165e-39d5-598d-8061-7d0c6882ab7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448808} {:answer/id #uuid "84f6c2b7-2f40-5d2c-9188-36685b487210" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448809}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "84f6c2b7-2f40-5d2c-9188-36685b487210" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186448809} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ed18b92-c4e7-57b6-b603-49203e853cfd" :answer-field/key "III" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186449444}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bd415fe2-3ead-5cbb-88ba-59d9d1273317" :question/math-academy-id "q-94740" :question/problem "Let $L$ be the set of all functions from $(0,\\infty )$ to $R$ of the form $f(x)=a{log}_{2}⁡(x),$ where $a\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** The scalar multiplication of functions in $L$ is associative.\n- **II.** For any $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R$ and $f(x)\\in L,$ we have $(\\alpha +\\beta )f(x)=\\alpha f(x)+\\beta f(x).$\n- **III.** For any $\\alpha \\in R$ and any $f(x),g(x)\\in L,$ we have $\\alpha (f(x)+g(x))=\\alpha f(x)+\\alpha g(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for any $f(x)\\in L$ and for any fixed $x\\in (0,\\infty ),$ the result of $f(x)$ is just a real number. Thus, for any $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\alpha (\\beta f(x))=(\\alpha \\beta )f(x).\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for any $f(x)\\in L$ and for any fixed $x\\in (0,\\infty ),$ the result of $f(x)$ is just a real number. Thus, for any $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n(\\alpha +\\beta )f(x)=\\alpha f(x)+\\beta f(x).\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, for any $f(x)\\in L$ and for any fixed $x\\in (0,\\infty ),$ the result of $f(x)$ is just a real number. Thus, for any $\\alpha \\in R,$ we obtain that \n\n$$\n\\alpha (f(x)+g(x))=\\alpha f(x)+\\alpha g(x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}]}
